    {
      "id": "zmZ1qthXNtl-1PzM3Ru0e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1741.94641487032,
      "y": -557.2140435994443,
      "width": 1823.8106666666683,
      "height": 1506.2120425029516,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ap",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1740500866,
      "version": 1508,
      "versionNonce": 206913059,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785268371,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. How many users ? How fast They are growing\n2. Is the App is read heavy or write heavy ?\n3. What data you dont wanna loss and what data you can afford to lose ?\n4. How much latency you can afford and in which section read or write ?\n5. What does it cost\n\nNow the right system design is less cost with answer of above 4 questions.\n\nAfter understanding the requirements, we can choose an appropriate architecture.\n\nTwo common approaches are:\n\nMonolithic architecture\nMicroservices architecture\n\nMonolithic Architecture\n\nIn a monolithic application, the different features of the application are developed and\ndeployed as one application/unit which means every service code like auth , Feed , like ,\ncomment is in one single server\n\nThe important point is that monolith does not necessarily mean one physical server or exactly\none database. A monolithic application can run on multiple servers for scalability.\n\nMicroservices Architecture\n\nIn a microservices architecture, the application is divided into smaller, independently deployable\nservices, where each service is responsible for a particular business capability.\n\nDistributed system → components of the system run across multiple machines and communicate\nover a network.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. How many users ? How fast They are growing\n2. Is the App is read heavy or write heavy ?\n3. What data you dont wanna loss and what data you can afford to lose ?\n4. How much latency you can afford and in which section read or write ?\n5. What does it cost\n\nNow the right system design is less cost with answer of above 4 questions.\n\nAfter understanding the requirements, we can choose an appropriate architecture.\n\nTwo common approaches are:\n\nMonolithic architecture\nMicroservices architecture\n\nMonolithic Architecture\n\nIn a monolithic application, the different features of the application are developed and deployed as one application/unit which means every service code like auth , Feed , like , comment is in one single server\n\nThe important point is that monolith does not necessarily mean one physical server or exactly one database. A monolithic application can run on multiple servers for scalability.\n\nMicroservices Architecture\n\nIn a microservices architecture, the application is divided into smaller, independently deployable services, where each service is responsible for a particular business capability.\n\nDistributed system → components of the system run across multiple machines and communicate over a network.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o7qcpa2qAhTSBMnYH0p9W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3996.94641487032,
      "y": -587.2140435994443,
      "width": 2753.810666666669,
      "height": 194.34994096812278,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 520713154,
      "version": 1666,
      "versionNonce": 1291361187,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785271279,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Microservices are useful when different parts of an application need to be developed, deployed, or scaled independently. We should not choose\nmicroservices simply because the application has a certain number of users.\n\nFor example, 1 lakh users/day does not automatically mean you need microservices. A well-designed monolith can handle significant traffic.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Microservices are useful when different parts of an application need to be developed, deployed, or scaled independently. We should not choose microservices simply because the application has a certain number of users.\n\nFor example, 1 lakh users/day does not automatically mean you need microservices. A well-designed monolith can handle significant traffic.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pseZYfJ5EgjaZmWlYTh11",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7088.226765187246,
      "y": -541.6018577256095,
      "width": 2803.8106666666695,
      "height": 1651.9744982290435,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 933557918,
      "version": 2073,
      "versionNonce": 315396269,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785276321,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When an application starts receiving more traffic, we need to increase its capacity. There are two common approaches:\n\nVertical Scaling\nHorizontal Scaling\n\n1. Vertical Scaling :- Vertical scaling means increasing the resources/specifications of an existing server. We are making the same server more\npowerful.\n\nProblems with Vertical Scaling\n  1. Hardware Limit :- There is a physical/instance limit to how much we can upgrade a single machine.You cannot keep increasing CPU and RAM\nforever.\n  2. Single Point of Failure :- If your entire application is running on one server, and that server goes down, the application goes down.\n  3. Cost :- Higher-spec machines can become increasingly expensive. For example, moving from a smaller instance to a much larger instance can cost\nsignificantly more than using several smaller instances.\n\n2. Horizontal Scaling :- Horizontal scaling means adding more servers/instances instead of making one server more powerful. The incoming requests\nare distributed among multiple servers.\n\nAdvantage with horizontal Scaling\n  1. Better Fault Tolerance :- If one server fails. The other servers can continue handling requests.\n  2. Can Handle Large Traffic :- Horizontal scaling is particularly useful when the number of requests becomes very large.\n  3. No Single Machine Limit :- Instead of being limited by the maximum capacity of one machine, we can add more machines.\n\nSometimes one powerful server is simpler, faster, and cheaper than managing 10 smaller servers. Horizontal scaling becomes attractive when the\nworkload, availability requirements, or growth make distributing the workload worthwhile.\n\nInterview Question :- \nYour application is getting slower. Why don't you just buy a bigger server?\"\n\nA good way to approach the answer is: First, understand the workload and bottleneck. If the application is relatively small and traffic is low,\nvertical scaling can be a simple solution because we can increase the server's resources without significantly changing the architecture.\n\nAs traffic grows, we may need horizontal scaling so that requests can be distributed across multiple servers and the system can provide better\navailability and capacity.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When an application starts receiving more traffic, we need to increase its capacity. There are two common approaches:\n\nVertical Scaling\nHorizontal Scaling\n\n1. Vertical Scaling :- Vertical scaling means increasing the resources/specifications of an existing server. We are making the same server more powerful.\n\nProblems with Vertical Scaling\n  1. Hardware Limit :- There is a physical/instance limit to how much we can upgrade a single machine.You cannot keep increasing CPU and RAM forever.\n  2. Single Point of Failure :- If your entire application is running on one server, and that server goes down, the application goes down.\n  3. Cost :- Higher-spec machines can become increasingly expensive. For example, moving from a smaller instance to a much larger instance can cost significantly more than using several smaller instances.\n\n2. Horizontal Scaling :- Horizontal scaling means adding more servers/instances instead of making one server more powerful. The incoming requests are distributed among multiple servers.\n\nAdvantage with horizontal Scaling\n  1. Better Fault Tolerance :- If one server fails. The other servers can continue handling requests.\n  2. Can Handle Large Traffic :- Horizontal scaling is particularly useful when the number of requests becomes very large.\n  3. No Single Machine Limit :- Instead of being limited by the maximum capacity of one machine, we can add more machines.\n\nSometimes one powerful server is simpler, faster, and cheaper than managing 10 smaller servers. Horizontal scaling becomes attractive when the workload, availability requirements, or growth make distributing the workload worthwhile.\n\nInterview Question :- \nYour application is getting slower. Why don't you just buy a bigger server?\"\n\nA good way to approach the answer is: First, understand the workload and bottleneck. If the application is relatively small and traffic is low, vertical scaling can be a simple solution because we can increase the server's resources without significantly changing the architecture.\n\nAs traffic grows, we may need horizontal scaling so that requests can be distributed across multiple servers and the system can provide better availability and capacity.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XexNtQAIRLn44E-FD1rl9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10092.393431853912,
      "y": -439.1018577256093,
      "width": 2803.8106666666695,
      "height": 1360.4495867768594,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aph",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1228675781,
      "version": 2342,
      "versionNonce": 45171811,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785297809,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Basically, when we send a request, we usually send it over HTTP, which is a stateless protocol (a set of rules for communication).\n\nStateless means that each request is treated independently. The server does not automatically remember previous requests.\n\nHTTP generally uses port 80 for unencrypted connections. The secure version is HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure)**, which uses\n**TLS** to encrypt the communication and generally uses port 443.\n\nNow let's say we want to open: https://google.com We can type google.com in the browser, but the network doesn't communicate using the domain\nname directly. It needs an IP address to know which machine to communicate with. So the browser uses DNS (Domain Name System) to resolve:\ngoogle.com → IP address. Once we get the IP address, the request can be sent toward that destination over the internet. But this is not the\nend.\n\nThe request also contains information such as:\n\n* Protocol → HTTPS\n* Destination → google.com\n* Port → 443\n* HTTP method → GET\n* Path → `/`\n* Headers\n* Other request data, if required\n\nSo : -\nDNS finds the destination server.\nThe IP address helps packets reach that destination.\nThe port identifies the service.\nThe HTTP method + path help the server determine the endpoint/resource.\nAnd finally, the server sends the HTTP response back to the client.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Basically, when we send a request, we usually send it over HTTP, which is a stateless protocol (a set of rules for communication).\n\nStateless means that each request is treated independently. The server does not automatically remember previous requests.\n\nHTTP generally uses port 80 for unencrypted connections. The secure version is HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure)**, which uses **TLS** to encrypt the communication and generally uses port 443.\n\nNow let's say we want to open: https://google.com We can type google.com in the browser, but the network doesn't communicate using the domain name directly. It needs an IP address to know which machine to communicate with. So the browser uses DNS (Domain Name System) to resolve: google.com → IP address. Once we get the IP address, the request can be sent toward that destination over the internet. But this is not the end.\n\nThe request also contains information such as:\n\n* Protocol → HTTPS\n* Destination → google.com\n* Port → 443\n* HTTP method → GET\n* Path → `/`\n* Headers\n* Other request data, if required\n\nSo : -\nDNS finds the destination server.\nThe IP address helps packets reach that destination.\nThe port identifies the service.\nThe HTTP method + path help the server determine the endpoint/resource.\nAnd finally, the server sends the HTTP response back to the client.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wQNgf4g27jbC5wjcmMLa9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10765.726765187246,
      "y": 1724.231475607724,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 485.874852420307,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1442547909,
      "version": 2579,
      "versionNonce": 1394013933,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785307532,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When we use horizontal scaling, we have multiple servers handling our application, so we need something\nto distribute incoming requests among those servers. This is where a Load Balancer comes in.\n\nA load balancer is software running on a server or network infrastructure whose job is to distribute\nincoming requests across available servers using different algorithms, such as Round Robin, Least\nConnections, and Weighted Distribution. \n\nHowever, if we have only one load balancer, it itself can become a single point of failure. Therefore, in\nlarge-scale systems, multiple load balancers or redundant load balancing infrastructure can be used so\nthat if one fails, another can continue handling the traffic.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When we use horizontal scaling, we have multiple servers handling our application, so we need something to distribute incoming requests among those servers. This is where a Load Balancer comes in.\n\nA load balancer is software running on a server or network infrastructure whose job is to distribute incoming requests across available servers using different algorithms, such as Round Robin, Least Connections, and Weighted Distribution. \n\nHowever, if we have only one load balancer, it itself can become a single point of failure. Therefore, in large-scale systems, multiple load balancers or redundant load balancing infrastructure can be used so that if one fails, another can continue handling the traffic.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pVrHw7oBqjq0w8p4RH1Ue",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8591.060098520578,
      "y": 1670.2314756077235,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 583.0498229043683,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 150469349,
      "version": 2727,
      "versionNonce": 341021005,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785307532,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A stateless system means that each request is independent and does not depend on information stored\non a particular server from a previous request. For example, if we have 10 servers, any request from a\nuser can go to any of those servers because the required information is available with the request itself\nor in a shared data store.\n\nOn the other hand, a stateful system means that the server needs information from previous requests\nto process the current request. For example, if a user's login state is stored only on Server 1, the load\nbalancer may need to keep sending that user's requests to Server 1. If Server 1 goes down or the\nrequest goes to another server, the user may face problems. To make the system stateless, we can\nstore authentication information in a JWT and send it with each request. Each server can then\nindependently verify the JWT using the required secret/key, so the request does not depend on which\nparticular server receives it.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A stateless system means that each request is independent and does not depend on information stored on a particular server from a previous request. For example, if we have 10 servers, any request from a user can go to any of those servers because the required information is available with the request itself or in a shared data store.\n\nOn the other hand, a stateful system means that the server needs information from previous requests to process the current request. For example, if a user's login state is stored only on Server 1, the load balancer may need to keep sending that user's requests to Server 1. If Server 1 goes down or the request goes to another server, the user may face problems. To make the system stateless, we can store authentication information in a JWT and send it with each request. Each server can then independently verify the JWT using the required secret/key, so the request does not depend on which particular server receives it.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1SGEzoAvueb7mBtu2T6jj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6221.060098520577,
      "y": 1732.231475607723,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 825.9872491145218,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2012488587,
      "version": 2857,
      "versionNonce": 1973899949,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "lnKwNt8JN_eOnByM33yL_",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1790785312329,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SQL vs NoSQL is not about what they can do. It's about which one makes the required workload easier,\nsafer, and more efficient given the trade-offs.\n\nNoSQL databases can often do the same business operation as SQL, but they don't necessarily provide\nthe same guarantees, query model, transaction model, or efficiency for that operation.\n\nSQL databases have traditionally been designed around relational data and strong transactional\nguarantees such as ACID transactions.\n\nNoSQL databases can also provide transactions and consistency features — modern NoSQL is much more\ncapable than the simple \"NoSQL = no transactions\" idea.\n\nE-commerce has important transactional operations such as orders, inventory, and payments. These\noperations have strong consistency and relationship requirements, so I would likely choose a relational\ndatabase for the transactional core. However, I could still use NoSQL for workloads such as product\ncatalogs, activity data, recommendations, or other areas where its data model and scaling\ncharacteristics are beneficial.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SQL vs NoSQL is not about what they can do. It's about which one makes the required workload easier, safer, and more efficient given the trade-offs.\n\nNoSQL databases can often do the same business operation as SQL, but they don't necessarily provide the same guarantees, query model, transaction model, or efficiency for that operation.\n\nSQL databases have traditionally been designed around relational data and strong transactional guarantees such as ACID transactions.\n\nNoSQL databases can also provide transactions and consistency features — modern NoSQL is much more capable than the simple \"NoSQL = no transactions\" idea.\n\nE-commerce has important transactional operations such as orders, inventory, and payments. These operations have strong consistency and relationship requirements, so I would likely choose a relational database for the transactional core. However, I could still use NoSQL for workloads such as product catalogs, activity data, recommendations, or other areas where its data model and scaling characteristics are beneficial.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q9bH2otKP_PqmkZLLeYvt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6211.282320742799,
      "y": 2935.231475607723,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 971.749704840614,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1640276748,
      "version": 3213,
      "versionNonce": 823583779,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785312027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The decision is more about data structure, relationships, consistency, query patterns, scale, and access\nrequirements.\n\nBefore choosing a database, we first understand the application's requirements and ask questions such\nas:\n\n1. ) What type of data are we storing? like user , posts , comments and likes etc\n2. ) What does the data structure look like? like How user schema look like\n3. ) Are there relationships between different types of data? like there is a relation between posts and\nuser\n4. ) What queries will we perform most often? which query we use more write or read \n5. ) How important are transactions and strong consistency? A transaction in SQL is a group of one or\nmore database operations that are treated as a single unit of work. Either all operations are\nsuccessfully completed, or none of them are applied. Transactions are commonly associated with the\nACID properties: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.\n6. ) How much data and traffic do we expect?\n7. ) Does the schema change frequently?\n\nAfter understanding these requirements, we design the data model/schema and choose an appropriate\ndatabase.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The decision is more about data structure, relationships, consistency, query patterns, scale, and access requirements.\n\nBefore choosing a database, we first understand the application's requirements and ask questions such as:\n\n1. ) What type of data are we storing? like user , posts , comments and likes etc\n2. ) What does the data structure look like? like How user schema look like\n3. ) Are there relationships between different types of data? like there is a relation between posts and user\n4. ) What queries will we perform most often? which query we use more write or read \n5. ) How important are transactions and strong consistency? A transaction in SQL is a group of one or more database operations that are treated as a single unit of work. Either all operations are successfully completed, or none of them are applied. Transactions are commonly associated with the ACID properties: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.\n6. ) How much data and traffic do we expect?\n7. ) Does the schema change frequently?\n\nAfter understanding these requirements, we design the data model/schema and choose an appropriate database.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hZLQdEY9Nxuajq5GfPA9x",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -965.3333984438027,
      "y": 6610.231475607723,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 825.9872491145218,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkh",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 386543648,
      "version": 4113,
      "versionNonce": 1973908512,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790880735181,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1.) LRU :- (Least Recently Used) is a cache eviction strategy used when the cache has limited space and\nbecomes full. When a new item needs to be stored, LRU removes the item that has been unused for the\nlongest period of time. Every time an item is accessed, we consider it recently used and move it toward\nthe \"recently used\" side.\n\nIn programming, an LRU Cache is commonly implemented using a HashMap + Doubly Linked List, where the\nHashMap provides fast lookup and the linked list keeps track of which items were used recently.\n\n2.) FIFO (First In, First Out):- FIFO removes the item that was added to the cache first. Think of it\nlike a queue: the first item that enters is the first item that leaves. For example, if the cache contains\nA → B → C in insertion order and the cache is full, adding D will remove A, even if A was recently\naccessed. FIFO is simple, but it does not consider how often or how recently an item is being used.\n\n3.) LFU (Least Frequently Used): LFU removes the item that has been used the fewest number of times.\nFor example, if A was accessed 10 times, B 5 times, and C only 1 time, then when the cache is full, LFU\nwill generally remove C. This is useful when frequently accessed data should stay in the cache, but it\nrequires keeping track of how many times each item has been accessed.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1.) LRU :- (Least Recently Used) is a cache eviction strategy used when the cache has limited space and becomes full. When a new item needs to be stored, LRU removes the item that has been unused for the longest period of time. Every time an item is accessed, we consider it recently used and move it toward the \"recently used\" side.\n\nIn programming, an LRU Cache is commonly implemented using a HashMap + Doubly Linked List, where the HashMap provides fast lookup and the linked list keeps track of which items were used recently.\n\n2.) FIFO (First In, First Out):- FIFO removes the item that was added to the cache first. Think of it like a queue: the first item that enters is the first item that leaves. For example, if the cache contains A → B → C in insertion order and the cache is full, adding D will remove A, even if A was recently accessed. FIFO is simple, but it does not consider how often or how recently an item is being used.\n\n3.) LFU (Least Frequently Used): LFU removes the item that has been used the fewest number of times. For example, if A was accessed 10 times, B 5 times, and C only 1 time, then when the cache is full, LFU will generally remove C. This is useful when frequently accessed data should stay in the cache, but it requires keeping track of how many times each item has been accessed.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "PKS-eu2JxMOVOVe2-yaAp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -950.2310761548952,
      "y": 7796.561045114408,
      "width": 2397.4141019482568,
      "height": 1117.512160566706,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apki",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1052725728,
      "version": 4327,
      "versionNonce": 2038507552,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790880738795,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When an application has only one database, that database can become a single point of failure and can also become a\nbottleneck as the number of users grows.\n\nTo handle more traffic, we can create multiple read replicas of the primary database. Read queries can then be distributed\nacross these replicas, allowing many users to read data simultaneously and reducing the load on the primary database.\n\nHowever, this creates a new problem: where should write operations happen?\n\nIf users could write to different replicas independently, the databases could contain different versions of the data and\nbecome inconsistent. Therefore, in a primary-replica architecture, write operations are usually sent to the primary database,\nwhile the replicas continuously copy changes from the primary. Because copying the changes takes some time, there can be a\ndelay called replication lag.\nDuring this period, a replica may return older data, which is why this architecture can provide eventual consistency.\nFor example, if a user updates their profile and immediately refreshes the page, their request might reach a replica that has\nnot received the update yet, causing the old profile to be displayed. One way to handle this is to route reads that require\nthe latest data to the primary database, at least for a period after the user's write.updates their profile and immediately\nrefreshes the page, their request might reach a replica that has not received the update yet, causing the old profile to be\ndisplayed. One way to handle this is to route reads that require the latest data to the primary database, at least for a\nperiod after the user's write.\n\nSuppose Primary DB dies then we promote one of replica DB to the Primary DB and also these replicas are also not our\nbackup suppose if someone runs command to delete all the data replcias simply copy the primary DB. so that's why we keep\nthe backup asides from the main system",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When an application has only one database, that database can become a single point of failure and can also become a bottleneck as the number of users grows.\n\nTo handle more traffic, we can create multiple read replicas of the primary database. Read queries can then be distributed across these replicas, allowing many users to read data simultaneously and reducing the load on the primary database.\n\nHowever, this creates a new problem: where should write operations happen?\n\nIf users could write to different replicas independently, the databases could contain different versions of the data and become inconsistent. Therefore, in a primary-replica architecture, write operations are usually sent to the primary database, while the replicas continuously copy changes from the primary. Because copying the changes takes some time, there can be a delay called replication lag.\nDuring this period, a replica may return older data, which is why this architecture can provide eventual consistency.\nFor example, if a user updates their profile and immediately refreshes the page, their request might reach a replica that has not received the update yet, causing the old profile to be displayed. One way to handle this is to route reads that require the latest data to the primary database, at least for a period after the user's write.updates their profile and immediately refreshes the page, their request might reach a replica that has not received the update yet, causing the old profile to be displayed. One way to handle this is to route reads that require the latest data to the primary database, at least for a period after the user's write.\n\nSuppose Primary DB dies then we promote one of replica DB to the Primary DB and also these replicas are also not our backup suppose if someone runs command to delete all the data replcias simply copy the primary DB. so that's why we keep the backup asides from the main system",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "YiozxWis0cVnt04Dym3De",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1958.6022571784388,
      "y": 6526.227711781075,
      "width": 2477.914101948256,
      "height": 2235.024321133412,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkiV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1164618208,
      "version": 4712,
      "versionNonce": 50178080,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790880756068,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When an application grows to millions of users, a single primary database may eventually become a bottleneck because it has to\nstore too much data and handle too many read and write operations. To solve this, we can use database sharding, where the\ndata is divided across multiple databases called shards.\n\nMethod 1: Modulo Based Distribution\n\nFor example, if we have three databases, we can calculate user_id % 3 to decide which database should store the user. \nIf user_id = 10, then 10 % 3 = 1, so the user goes to DB-1. \nIf user_id = 11, then 11 % 3 = 2, so the user goes to DB-2. \nHowever, this approach creates a major problem when we add or remove a database. Suppose we initially have three databases\nand user_id = 10 goes to DB-1 because 10 % 3 = 1. Now we add a fourth database and change the calculation to user_id % 4.\n10 % 4 = 2, so the same user now maps to DB-2.\nThis happens to a large portion of the users, meaning the system may need to move a huge amount of data between databases.\nThis can cause high network traffic, increased load, migration time, cache invalidation, and operational complexity. Removing a\ndatabase can cause the same problem.\n\nMethod 2: Consistent Hashing\n\nwe can use consistent hashing. Instead of directly using the number of databases in a modulo operation, we imagine a circular\nhash ring, for example from 0 to 99.\nWe place each database at a position on the ring:\n\nDB-A at 0, \nDB-B at 25, \nDB-C at 50, \nDB-D at 75\n\nWe then pass each user's ID through a hash function. Suppose hash(12345) = 42. We start at position 42 and move clockwise\nuntil we find the first database. The first database is DB-C at position 50, so user 12345 is stored on DB-C.\nNow suppose we add a new database, DB-E, at position 37. Only the keys whose hash positions fall between the previous\ndatabase position and the new database position need to move to DB-E. In this example, keys in the relevant range between\nDB-B and DB-E (which is 26 to 37) can move to DB-E, while most of the data remains where it was.\nTherefore, adding a database does not require remapping every user. This greatly reduces data migration, network traffic, and\nthe operational cost of scaling.\n\nHowever, there is another problem called uneven distribution. If we place each physical database only once on the ring, some\ndatabases may receive much more data or traffic than others depending on where the hash values happen to fall.\nTo solve this, we use virtual nodes.\nInstead of placing each physical database at only one position, we place it at multiple positions on the ring. \nFor example :- \nDB-A might have virtual nodes at 0, 40, 80, DB-B at 25, 65,\nDB-C at 13, 50, 90, \nDB-D at 75\n\nThese positions are called virtual nodes, but they still represent the same physical database. Because each database appears at\nmultiple locations, the hash space is distributed more evenly, helping balance both data and traffic across the databases.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When an application grows to millions of users, a single primary database may eventually become a bottleneck because it has to store too much data and handle too many read and write operations. To solve this, we can use database sharding, where the data is divided across multiple databases called shards.\n\nMethod 1: Modulo Based Distribution\n\nFor example, if we have three databases, we can calculate user_id % 3 to decide which database should store the user. \nIf user_id = 10, then 10 % 3 = 1, so the user goes to DB-1. \nIf user_id = 11, then 11 % 3 = 2, so the user goes to DB-2. \nHowever, this approach creates a major problem when we add or remove a database. Suppose we initially have three databases and user_id = 10 goes to DB-1 because 10 % 3 = 1. Now we add a fourth database and change the calculation to user_id % 4. 10 % 4 = 2, so the same user now maps to DB-2.\nThis happens to a large portion of the users, meaning the system may need to move a huge amount of data between databases. This can cause high network traffic, increased load, migration time, cache invalidation, and operational complexity. Removing a database can cause the same problem.\n\nMethod 2: Consistent Hashing\n\nwe can use consistent hashing. Instead of directly using the number of databases in a modulo operation, we imagine a circular hash ring, for example from 0 to 99.\nWe place each database at a position on the ring:\n\nDB-A at 0, \nDB-B at 25, \nDB-C at 50, \nDB-D at 75\n\nWe then pass each user's ID through a hash function. Suppose hash(12345) = 42. We start at position 42 and move clockwise until we find the first database. The first database is DB-C at position 50, so user 12345 is stored on DB-C.\nNow suppose we add a new database, DB-E, at position 37. Only the keys whose hash positions fall between the previous database position and the new database position need to move to DB-E. In this example, keys in the relevant range between DB-B and DB-E (which is 26 to 37) can move to DB-E, while most of the data remains where it was.\nTherefore, adding a database does not require remapping every user. This greatly reduces data migration, network traffic, and the operational cost of scaling.\n\nHowever, there is another problem called uneven distribution. If we place each physical database only once on the ring, some databases may receive much more data or traffic than others depending on where the hash values happen to fall.\nTo solve this, we use virtual nodes.\nInstead of placing each physical database at only one position, we place it at multiple positions on the ring. \nFor example :- \nDB-A might have virtual nodes at 0, 40, 80, DB-B at 25, 65,\nDB-C at 13, 50, 90, \nDB-D at 75\n\nThese positions are called virtual nodes, but they still represent the same physical database. Because each database appears at multiple locations, the hash space is distributed more evenly, helping balance both data and traffic across the databases.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "nwqV9uQH4-KgJnDRFucwW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -967.2119887692172,
      "y": 5343.705048031778,
      "width": 1987.5406972589358,
      "height": 923.1622195985832,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1232002528,
      "version": 3955,
      "versionNonce": 1298210272,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790877873502,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The main idea is that JavaScript executes synchronous code one operation at a time, so even if two\nrequests arrive at almost exactly the same time, their JavaScript code does not execute literally at\nthe same instant because it maintain a stack.\n\nSuppose two requests for the same data arrive while the cache is empty. Request 1 checks the cache,\nfinds a miss, creates the database request, and immediately stores its Promise in the inFlight Map\nbefore yielding with await. \n\nOnly after that can Request 2 execute its code. Request 2 checks the cache, still sees a miss, but\nnow checks inFlight and finds the Promise created by Request 1, so it does not create another\ndatabase request; it simply waits for the same Promise. \n\nWhile the DB request is running, Node.js can handle other incoming requests because the DB operation\nis I/O and doesn't block the JavaScript thread. Once the DB responds, the Promise resolves, the data\nis stored in the cache, and all the requests waiting on that same Promise receive the result. \n\nThe important part is that the check and storing of the Promise happen synchronously without an\nawait in between, which prevents two requests from both thinking that no request is currently in\nflight.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The main idea is that JavaScript executes synchronous code one operation at a time, so even if two requests arrive at almost exactly the same time, their JavaScript code does not execute literally at the same instant because it maintain a stack.\n\nSuppose two requests for the same data arrive while the cache is empty. Request 1 checks the cache, finds a miss, creates the database request, and immediately stores its Promise in the inFlight Map before yielding with await. \n\nOnly after that can Request 2 execute its code. Request 2 checks the cache, still sees a miss, but now checks inFlight and finds the Promise created by Request 1, so it does not create another database request; it simply waits for the same Promise. \n\nWhile the DB request is running, Node.js can handle other incoming requests because the DB operation is I/O and doesn't block the JavaScript thread. Once the DB responds, the Promise resolves, the data is stored in the cache, and all the requests waiting on that same Promise receive the result. \n\nThe important part is that the check and storing of the Promise happen synchronously without an await in between, which prevents two requests from both thinking that no request is currently in flight.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "KpO4OfQjP_6dm7_TnfX2r",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3821.0600985205774,
      "y": 1783.0648089410565,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 923.1622195985832,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1602722188,
      "version": 2952,
      "versionNonce": 1636404579,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785322017,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Indexing is a technique used in databases to make reading and searching data faster. We can create an\nindex on a column that we frequently use for searching, filtering, sorting, or joining.\n\nUnder the hood, databases commonly use a B-tree or B+ tree to maintain the index, which allows the\ndatabase to find the required data without scanning every row of the table.\n\nA primary key is automatically indexed in most SQL databases, while a foreign key is not necessarily\nindexed automatically, so we may need to create an index explicitly if we frequently query using that\ncolumn.\n\nFor example, in an Instagram-like application, if the posts table has a user_id foreign key and we\nfrequently fetch all posts belonging to a user, we can create an index on user_id to make that query\nfaster.\n\nHowever, indexes also have a cost: they require additional storage and can make INSERT, UPDATE, and\nDELETE operations slower because the database also needs to maintain the index whenever the\nunderlying data changes.\n\nTherefore, we should create indexes based on actual query patterns rather than indexing every column.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Indexing is a technique used in databases to make reading and searching data faster. We can create an index on a column that we frequently use for searching, filtering, sorting, or joining.\n\nUnder the hood, databases commonly use a B-tree or B+ tree to maintain the index, which allows the database to find the required data without scanning every row of the table.\n\nA primary key is automatically indexed in most SQL databases, while a foreign key is not necessarily indexed automatically, so we may need to create an index explicitly if we frequently query using that column.\n\nFor example, in an Instagram-like application, if the posts table has a user_id foreign key and we frequently fetch all posts belonging to a user, we can create an index on user_id to make that query faster.\n\nHowever, indexes also have a cost: they require additional storage and can make INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations slower because the database also needs to maintain the index whenever the underlying data changes.\n\nTherefore, we should create indexes based on actual query patterns rather than indexing every column.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hEWi_GU0SkIpFFF7kjZr8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1113.0466181370234,
      "y": 1668.7097400222642,
      "width": 2397.5827247847355,
      "height": 1068.9246753246753,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1728063853,
      "version": 3314,
      "versionNonce": 1669611523,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785343919,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Caching is a technique where we store frequently accessed data in a fast storage layer, usually RAM, so that we don't have\nto query the main database every time. Since accessing RAM is much faster than accessing disk/SSD-based storage, a cache\ncan significantly reduce response time. We generally don't store the entire database in the cache because RAM is much more\nexpensive and limited compared to SSD/HDD storage. Instead, we store frequently requested or expensive-to-compute data.\n\nA cache sits in front of the database. When the requested data is found in the cache, it is called a Cache Hit, and we\nreturn the data directly from the cache. If the data isn't present, it is called a Cache Miss; we then fetch the data from\nthe database and can store it in the cache for future requests.\n\nFor example, in an Instagram-like application, posts from extremely popular users may receive millions of requests. Keeping\nsuch frequently accessed posts in the cache can reduce the load on the main database. However, this can introduce a\nconsistency problem. If the original data is deleted or updated in the database, users may still receive the old data from\nthe cache.\n\nTo handle this, we can use TTL (Time To Live), which defines how long an item should remain in the cache. For example, with\na TTL of 60 seconds, the cached data automatically expires after 60 seconds. After expiration, the next request can\nfetch the latest data from the database and put it back into the cache.\n\nOne problem with expiration is Cache Stampede (or Thundering Herd). If a very popular cache entry expires and millions of\nrequests arrive at almost the same time, they may all miss the cache and hit the database simultaneously, creating a huge\nload. Techniques such as TTL jitter (randomizing expiration times), request coalescing, and locking can help prevent this\nproblem.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Caching is a technique where we store frequently accessed data in a fast storage layer, usually RAM, so that we don't have to query the main database every time. Since accessing RAM is much faster than accessing disk/SSD-based storage, a cache can significantly reduce response time. We generally don't store the entire database in the cache because RAM is much more expensive and limited compared to SSD/HDD storage. Instead, we store frequently requested or expensive-to-compute data.\n\nA cache sits in front of the database. When the requested data is found in the cache, it is called a Cache Hit, and we return the data directly from the cache. If the data isn't present, it is called a Cache Miss; we then fetch the data from the database and can store it in the cache for future requests.\n\nFor example, in an Instagram-like application, posts from extremely popular users may receive millions of requests. Keeping such frequently accessed posts in the cache can reduce the load on the main database. However, this can introduce a consistency problem. If the original data is deleted or updated in the database, users may still receive the old data from the cache.\n\nTo handle this, we can use TTL (Time To Live), which defines how long an item should remain in the cache. For example, with a TTL of 60 seconds, the cached data automatically expires after 60 seconds. After expiration, the next request can fetch the latest data from the database and put it back into the cache.\n\nOne problem with expiration is Cache Stampede (or Thundering Herd). If a very popular cache entry expires and millions of requests arrive at almost the same time, they may all miss the cache and hit the database simultaneously, creating a huge load. Techniques such as TTL jitter (randomizing expiration times), request coalescing, and locking can help prevent this problem.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "707efkJi4Jx-UjWsFZvcI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -913.6029391867289,
      "y": 2115.516836533684,
      "width": 1761.0157964391594,
      "height": 583.0498229043683,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkv",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 656720352,
      "version": 3672,
      "versionNonce": 1714123808,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790876369995,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Eventual consistency means that after data is updated, different servers or replicas may\ntemporarily have different versions of the data, but they will eventually become consistent.\nFor example, if you update your profile name, one server might show the new name while\nanother still shows the old name for a short period. This is useful when high availability\nand performance are more important than getting the newest data immediately.\n\nStrong consistency, on the other hand, means that once a write is completed, every\nsubsequent read should see the latest version of the data. There should be no period\nwhere one server returns old data after the update has been confirmed. Strong\nconsistency gives more predictable and accurate reads, but it can require more coordination\nbetween servers and may reduce performance or availability compared with eventual\nconsistency.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Eventual consistency means that after data is updated, different servers or replicas may temporarily have different versions of the data, but they will eventually become consistent. For example, if you update your profile name, one server might show the new name while another still shows the old name for a short period. This is useful when high availability and performance are more important than getting the newest data immediately.\n\nStrong consistency, on the other hand, means that once a write is completed, every subsequent read should see the latest version of the data. There should be no period where one server returns old data after the update has been confirmed. Strong consistency gives more predictable and accurate reads, but it can require more coordination between servers and may reduce performance or availability compared with eventual consistency.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HkA-gouLSFqH2SMTuZkVm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1105.5466181370234,
