      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790792966664,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q ) Instagram has millions of users, but only some users, such as celebrities or popular creators, receive a very large number\nof requests. So, do we keep only these highly popular users' data in the cache while allowing normal users' data to be\nfetched directly from the database? If normal users are not cached, wouldn't millions of requests from those users create a\nhuge load on the database?\n\nWe don't cache only celebrities. Instead, we mainly cache **hot data**, which means data that is requested very frequently.\nFor example, a celebrity's profile may receive thousands of requests per second, so keeping it in Redis prevents the\ndatabase from handling all those requests. A normal user's profile might receive only a few requests, so it may not need to\nstay in the cache. With the **cache-aside approach**, even a normal user's data can be added to the cache when it is\nrequested frequently. If the data is not accessed for a while, its TTL expires or it can be removed through an eviction\npolicy. So, the database stores all the data, while the cache keeps the frequently accessed data to reduce database load\nand improve response time.\n",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q ) Instagram has millions of users, but only some users, such as celebrities or popular creators, receive a very large number of requests. So, do we keep only these highly popular users' data in the cache while allowing normal users' data to be fetched directly from the database? If normal users are not cached, wouldn't millions of requests from those users create a huge load on the database?\n\nWe don't cache only celebrities. Instead, we mainly cache **hot data**, which means data that is requested very frequently. For example, a celebrity's profile may receive thousands of requests per second, so keeping it in Redis prevents the database from handling all those requests. A normal user's profile might receive only a few requests, so it may not need to stay in the cache. With the **cache-aside approach**, even a normal user's data can be added to the cache when it is requested frequently. If the data is not accessed for a while, its TTL expires or it can be removed through an eviction policy. So, the database stores all the data, while the cache keeps the frequently accessed data to reduce database load and improve response time.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OqE-6pR7rA4BiO683rHy1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3985.279748203653,
      "y": -335.5473769327775,
      "width": 2763.8106666666686,
      "height": 485.874852420307,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1408883778,
      "version": 1842,
      "versionNonce": 1732980547,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785271279,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reason 1 — Independent Scaling\n\nSuppose we have an Instagram like application and in this type of app Reading posts happens much more frequently than Writing posts happens\nmuch less frequently.\n\nWith monolith :- If the read traffic increases significantly, we may need to add more instances of the whole application. The problem is that\nwe're also replicating the parts responsible for writing and other functionality, even though they might not need additional capacity.\n\nWith Microservices :- We can separate the application into services Now, if read traffic increases, we can scale only the Read Service. This is\ncalled independent scaling.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Reason 1 — Independent Scaling\n\nSuppose we have an Instagram like application and in this type of app Reading posts happens much more frequently than Writing posts happens much less frequently.\n\nWith monolith :- If the read traffic increases significantly, we may need to add more instances of the whole application. The problem is that we're also replicating the parts responsible for writing and other functionality, even though they might not need additional capacity.\n\nWith Microservices :- We can separate the application into services Now, if read traffic increases, we can scale only the Read Service. This is called independent scaling.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5we-iQDqP3KB-9VCAmXeu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3985.279748203653,
      "y": 181.11928973388922,
      "width": 2763.8106666666686,
      "height": 388.69988193624556,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 980770562,
      "version": 1956,
      "versionNonce": 759844579,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785271279,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reason 2 — Large Teams\n\nMicroservices can also make sense when an organization has many teams working on different parts of the system. For example: Team A → User\nService , Team B → Post Service \nEach team can own and deploy its service independently. With a huge monolithic codebase, many teams working on the same codebase can lead\nto: More coordination , More merge conflicts , More dependencies between teams , More difficult deployments. \nMicroservices can help teams work more independently. However, team size alone doesn't automatically mean you need microservices. A modular\nmonolith can also solve many of these problems.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Reason 2 — Large Teams\n\nMicroservices can also make sense when an organization has many teams working on different parts of the system. For example: Team A → User Service , Team B → Post Service \nEach team can own and deploy its service independently. With a huge monolithic codebase, many teams working on the same codebase can lead to: More coordination , More merge conflicts , More dependencies between teams , More difficult deployments. \nMicroservices can help teams work more independently. However, team size alone doesn't automatically mean you need microservices. A modular monolith can also solve many of these problems.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2YzL6EPf2jRdDFJiOeK2O",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3991.113081536986,
      "y": 594.4526230672225,
      "width": 2763.8106666666686,
      "height": 583.0498229043683,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1820243550,
      "version": 2071,
      "versionNonce": 293894787,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790785271279,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Downsides of Microservices\n\nMicroservices give us flexibility, but they also introduce complexity.\n\n1. Network Communication :- In a monolith: Service A → Service B may simply be an in-process function call. In microservices: Service A -->\nNetwork --> Service B The communication happens over a network, which introduces latency, network failures, retries, timeouts, etc.\n\n2. More Infrastructure :- Instead of managing one application, we may have to manage: Multiple services , Multiple deployments , Service-to-\nservice communication , Monitoring , Logging , Service discovery , Network configuration\nIf something fails, we need to determine which service failed and how the request moved through the system.\n\nSo microservices can significantly increase operational complexity.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Downsides of Microservices\n\nMicroservices give us flexibility, but they also introduce complexity.\n\n1. Network Communication :- In a monolith: Service A → Service B may simply be an in-process function call. In microservices: Service A --> Network --> Service B The communication happens over a network, which introduces latency, network failures, retries, timeouts, etc.\n\n2. More Infrastructure :- Instead of managing one application, we may have to manage: Multiple services , Multiple deployments , Service-to-service communication , Monitoring , Logging , Service discovery , Network configuration\nIf something fails, we need to determine which service failed and how the request moved through the system.\n\nSo microservices can significantly increase operational complexity.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
